  {
    "sha": "3c349bf705f5ce1356cba7a6338b4a6238039b23",
    "node_id": "C_kwDOU85DiNoAKDNjMzQ5YmY3MDVmNWNlMTM1NmNiYTdhNjMzOGI0YTYyMzgwMzliMjM",
    "commit": {
      "author": {
        "name": "shruxx",
        "email": "24369531+shruxx@users.noreply.github.com",
        "date": "2026-10-06T08:40:17Z"
      },
      "committer": {
        "name": "shruxx",
        "email": "24369531+shruxx@users.noreply.github.com",
        "date": "2026-10-06T08:40:17Z"
      },
      "message": "engine: warm up with enough tokens to matter for a MoE\n\nThe warm-up generated a single token, which for a mixture-of-experts\npages in almost nothing: Kolibri-1 routes each token to 6 of its 384\nexperts per layer, so one token touches well under a percent of the\nweights left on the CPU side.  It now generates 128.\n\nThat is still not all of them.  Measured on a 48 GB M5 Pro, Kolibri-1\nQ3_K_M at 25 of 51 layers, after a load with the page cache churned by\nan unrelated 42 GB download, generation climbed over six real requests:\n\n  21.5, 22.9, 32.5, 30.0, 32.2, 31.0 tok/s\n  first token 2.0, 1.7, 1.2, 1.3, 1.1, 1.2 s\n\nSo the honest claim is that the warm-up takes the worst off the first\nreply, not that it makes a split behave like a resident model.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>",
